# What must not be uploaded to the build.
#
# Two of these are not housekeeping.
#
# `hr/` is this project's virtualenv, at 1.3 GB. The conventional `venv/`,
# `.venv/` and `env/` lines miss it because it is named after the project, and
# without this line every deploy uploads 1.3 GB before a single instruction
# runs. The same line is in `.dockerignore` for the same reason.
#
# `data/*.db` is the audit log: every employee question and the answer they
# were given, which `/api/audit` is admin-gated to protect. It must not be
# uploaded into a build artefact. On Vercel the databases live in /tmp anyway,
# so nothing here needs them.

hr/
venv/
.venv/
env/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

.git/
.github/
.claude/

# Secrets. Vercel takes configuration from its own environment variables; a
# .env in the build would silently outrank them in some setups and is a secret
# in an artefact either way.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The audit log and any other local database.
data/*.db
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm
uploads/

# Development-only surfaces.
tests/
scripts/
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
create_project.py
ingest_sample_kb.py
run.py

# Documentation and reference material. The console carries its own copy of
# everything a visitor needs to read, so a 2 MB PDF in the bundle buys nothing.
#
# **The leading slashes are load-bearing, and the lack of them was a bug.**
# `.vercelignore` follows gitignore semantics, where an unanchored `*.md`
# matches at *every* depth -- so it silently excluded all eleven policy
# documents in `data/private_kb/`, and the deployment answered every question
# with "insufficient evidence" in three milliseconds. Nothing in the build log
# mentions it; the knowledge base is simply not there.
#
# The identical pattern is safe in `.dockerignore` because Docker's `*` does
# not cross a path separator, so a root-level `*.md` there means only
# root-level. Two files, two meanings, one pattern. Anchor it here.
/*.md
!/README.md
/docs/*.md
/docs/*.pdf
/docs/*.png

# The corpus is what this answers from. Stated positively as well as left
# un-ignored above, because it is the one directory whose absence turns a
# working deployment into a silent one.
!data/private_kb/
!data/private_kb/**
